---
import { Picture, getImage } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import EnquiryForm from '../components/EnquiryForm.astro';
import VideoEmbed from '../components/VideoEmbed.astro';
import intAboveClouds from '../assets/photos/int-above-clouds.jpg';
import portrait from '../assets/photos/portrait-4x5.jpg';
import notebook from '../assets/figures/mentorship-colab.png';
import notebookPlot from '../assets/figures/mentorship-colab-plot.png';

// The whole notebook cell, code and plot, for the link under the cropped plot.
const notebookFull = await getImage({ src: notebook, format: 'webp', quality: 90 });

// The page's title, description and link preview never mention the university.
const title = 'Exoplanet Research Mentorship · Dr Matthew Hooton';
const description =
  'A 14-session online research course in which a school student looks for an unconfirmed planet in NASA TESS data and writes it up as a paper. Designed and taught by Dr Matthew Hooton.';
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogImage="/og/mentorship.jpg"
  ogImageAlt="The Isaac Newton Telescope above the clouds at sunset, with the words Exoplanet Research Mentorship"
  footer="mentorship"
>
  <div class="m-hero">
    <div class="m-hero-media">
      <Picture
        src={intAboveClouds}
        alt="The Isaac Newton Telescope's dome above a sea of clouds at sunset on La Palma"
        formats={['avif', 'webp']}
        widths={[640, 960, 1280, 1600, 2000, 2400]}
        sizes="100vw"
        priority
      />
    </div>
    <div class="m-hero-text">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Exoplanet Research Mentorship</p>
        <h1>Discover a new exoplanet</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          My own independent online research course. NASA's TESS space telescope has flagged
          thousands of possible planets that no one has yet checked. Each student takes a set of
          these stars and, over fourteen one-hour online sessions, does the checking that turns a
          candidate into a planet.
        </p>
        <p class="by">Designed and taught by Dr Matthew Hooton, astrophysicist.</p>
        <a class="btn" href="#enquire">Enquire</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="credit">
      The Isaac Newton Telescope above the clouds, La Palma.
    </p>
    <nav class="subnav" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#why-real">Why this is real research</a>
      <a href="#gains">What your student gains</a>
      <a href="#outcomes">Outcomes</a>
      <a href="#who-teaches">Who teaches</a>
      <a href="#course">The course</a>
      <a href="#practicalities">Practicalities</a>
      <a href="#enquire">Enquire</a>
    </nav>

    <section class="block" id="why-real" aria-labelledby="why-h">
      <div class="why">
        <div class="why-text">
          <h2 id="why-h">Why this is real research</h2>
          <p>
            An exoplanet is a planet that orbits a star other than the Sun. The first was found in
            the 1990s. Most of the planets known today were found by watching for a small, regular
            dimming of a star's light as a planet passes in front of it; a graph of the star's
            brightness over time is called a light curve.
          </p>
          <p>
            Since 2018, TESS has found almost 10,000 such signals. Each one is only a candidate until
            it has been checked with further observations, because two stars orbiting each other
            can produce a similar dimming. Fewer than half of these candidates have been checked,
            because there are not enough astrophysicists to do it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <figure>
          <VideoEmbed
            id="xNeRqbw18Jk"
            title="ESA animation of a planet passing in front of its star, with the light curve traced out"
          />
          <figcaption class="cap">
            A planet passing in front of its star, with the dip in starlight traced out as a light
            curve. Source: ESA.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="gains" aria-labelledby="gains-h">
      <h2 id="gains-h">What your student gains</h2>
      <div class="gains">
        <article>
          <h3>Coding</h3>
          <p>
            They learn to write scientific Python, and run their own programs on data from a NASA
            space telescope. Python is the main programming language of astrophysics and data
            science.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Research</h3>
          <p>
            They work on a question whose answer nobody yet knows. They learn to read the research
            literature, and to rule out false alarms in imperfect data. Above all, they learn to
            reason about statistics and uncertainty, which is the biggest shift between learning
            science and doing it.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Writing and presenting</h3>
          <p>
            Each week they talk me through their results. They come away with a complete research
            project, written up as a paper, that they understand in every detail and can defend in
            an interview. Afterwards they are free to submit it to a journal, a conference or a
            science fair, and I advise on where.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="outcomes" aria-labelledby="outcomes-h">
      <h2 id="outcomes-h">Outcomes</h2>
      <p class="section-text">
        I began teaching this course in late 2025. Papers from it have so far produced three journal
        submissions and, in 2026,
        <a href="https://indico.global/event/16926/contributions/163975/">a poster selected for presentation at the Australian Institute of Physics Congress</a>,
        Australia's national physics conference. Former students have continued their research with
        me beyond the course, and one has entered a national science competition with that work.
      </p>
      <figure class="quote">
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            “By the end, I had learned a huge amount about both the science and the research
            process, and producing a complete paper made the experience feel genuinely worthwhile.”
          </p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption class="cap">— Former student, 2026</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="who-teaches" aria-labelledby="who-h">
      <div class="who">
        <figure>
          <Picture
            src={portrait}
            alt="Matthew Hooton"
            formats={['avif', 'webp']}
            widths={[320, 480, 640, 960]}
            sizes="(max-width: 760px) min(18rem, calc(100vw - 40px)), 300px"
          />
        </figure>
        <div class="who-text">
          <h2 id="who-h">Who teaches</h2>
          <p>
            I am an astrophysicist at the University of Cambridge, in the group of Professor Didier
            Queloz, who shared the 2019 Nobel Prize in Physics for discovering the first planet
            orbiting a star like the Sun. I lead a programme of observations with the James Webb
            Space Telescope, am on the science team of the CHEOPS space telescope, co-supervise a PhD
            student, and have published more than 80 papers, including in <i>Nature</i> and
            <i>Science</i>.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is my own independent course. I designed it and teach every session myself, and I
            take only a few students each year.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>


    <section class="block" id="course" aria-labelledby="course-h">
      <h2 id="course-h">The course</h2>
      <div class="course">
        <div class="course-text">
          <div class="phase">
            <p class="when">Sessions 1–6</p>
            <div>
              <h3>Foundations</h3>
              <p>
                How planets are found and measured from the dimming of their stars, what real TESS
                data looks like, how a planet search works, and how a research paper is written.
                Taught through interactive notebooks the student runs and changes.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="phase">
            <p class="when">Sessions 7–14</p>
            <div>
              <h3>The research</h3>
              <p>
                Three stages, each building on the last, with the paper written alongside, section
                by section. These are the steps a professional astrophysicist follows.
              </p>
              <ol class="steps">
                <li>
                  <span class="n">1</span>
                  <h4 class="s">Search</h4>
                  <p>
                    They write their own code to find the signals in the TESS data for 20 stars, and
                    narrow them down to two candidates.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <span class="n">2</span>
                  <h4 class="s">Check</h4>
                  <p>
                    They analyse images from ground-based telescopes to rule out the eclipsing stars
                    that can mimic a planet.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <span class="n">3</span>
                  <h4 class="s">Measure</h4>
                  <p>
                    They fit a statistical model to measure the planet's size and orbit with proper
                    uncertainties.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="nb">
          <Picture
            src={notebookPlot}
            alt="A plot from a course notebook: transit light curves that get longer as the planet's orbit gets wider"
            formats={['webp']}
            quality={90}
            widths={[400, 600, 742]}
            sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 40px), 742px"
          />
          <figcaption class="cap">
            From a course notebook: how a planet's distance from its star changes the length of its
            transit. <a href={notebookFull.src}>See the whole notebook cell</a>, with the Python code
            that makes the plot.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p class="endline">The paper is theirs: they write it themselves, and it carries their name.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="practicalities" aria-labelledby="prac-h">
      <h2 id="prac-h">Practicalities</h2>
      <dl class="prac">
        <dt>Sessions</dt>
        <dd>
          One-hour online sessions, once a week. The start date and the time are agreed with each
          family; I am in the UK and teach at times that suit students in the United States.
        </dd>
        <dt>Format</dt>
        <dd>One-to-one, or in a small group of two or three students.</dd>
        <dt>Between sessions</dt>
        <dd>
          Four to six hours of independent work a week in Google Colab, in the browser with nothing
          to install. Between sessions, I am an email away.
        </dd>
        <dt>Who it is for</dt>
        <dd>
          Students in the final years of high school or the early years of an undergraduate degree
          who are curious about physics or astrophysics and comfortable with basic Python:
          variables, loops and functions. No experience of scientific programming is needed; the
          course builds it.
        </dd>
        <dt>Parents</dt>
        <dd>
          Parents of school students are welcome at any session, and are kept informed of progress.
        </dd>
        <dt>Terms</dt>
        <dd>Written terms, on a single page, are agreed with parents before the course begins.</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="enquire" aria-labelledby="enq-h">
      <h2 id="enq-h">Enquire</h2>
      <p class="form-intro">
        I take only a few students each year. For fees and availability, tell me a little about your
        student and I'll reply within 48 hours to arrange a short conversation.
      </p>
      <EnquiryForm />
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
